course: dictionary
lesson: 7446
title: Spotting a pompous character
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you what the writer thinks of a character. Pompous is one of these judging words. A pompous character acts too important and too serious, and uses grand words to show off.
explain: Look for clues in what the character does and says. Do they boast about themselves? Do they use long, fancy words when simple ones would do? Does the writer seem to be teasing them? In The Wind in the Willows, Toad is pompous and boastful, so the reader is meant to smile at his big opinions of himself.
explain: Pompous can also describe writing or speech. «A silly, cheerful scene that pokes fun at pompous meetings and races with no rules» shows the writer laughing at people who take themselves far too seriously. Check it by asking: could the same thing be said in plain words? If not, the big words may be showing off.
fact: Pompous means acting too important and too serious, using grand words to show off.
fact: Pompous can also describe speech or writing that is full of big, showy words.
fact: Pompous is a judging word: it tells you what the writer thinks of a character.
fact: Toad in The Wind in the Willows and the Duchess and the King of Hearts in Alice in Wonderland are both pompous characters.
fact: Pompous is stronger than simply proud, because it adds the idea of showing off.
exercise: What does pompous mean? | *Acting too important and using grand words to show off | Very small and quiet | Happy to be outdoors | Ready to share everything with others
exercise: «A silly, cheerful scene that pokes fun at pompous meetings and races with no rules» What does pokes fun at mean? | Thanks warmly for helping | *Teases in a light, joking way | Copies exactly | Hides away from
exercise: The Duchess in Alice in Wonderland is pompous. What does this tell the reader? | The writer admires her wisdom completely | The writer wants the reader to feel sorry for her | *The writer thinks she is self-important and a bit silly | The writer thinks she is shy and gentle
test: Which sentence uses pompous language? | *Allow me to elucidate the matter, for I am a person of considerable importance | We went to the park today | I like cheese on toast | Please pass the salt
test: Pompous can describe a speech. What might make a speech pompous? | *Big, showy words used to show off | Its loud, noisy start | The speaker's shoes | Its length in minutes only
exam: Toad in The Wind in the Willows is pompous. Which judgement fits best? | Kind and helpful to every animal he meets | Quiet and shy, so the reader should feel sorry for him | Lost and frightened in the wood | *Boastful and self-important, so the reader is meant to smile at him
